# GBNF grammar for the lisptc (.ptc) Lisp dialect.
# Derived from the reader in packages/interpreter/src/lisp.ts, whose tokeniser is
#   /\s+|("(\\.?|.)*?"|,@?|[^()'`~" \t]+|.)/g
# Only the parenthesised forms are program text; the free text around them is
# prose the interpreter ignores (stripProse). Prose may not contain a paren,
# which is exactly what keeps the model from emitting an unbalanced one: a lone
# ")" — as in ":)" — is unsayable, and every "(" it opens must be closed as a
# real form. The dialect has no comment syntax; prose replaces it.

# A reply may hold no form at all: pure prose is a program that does nothing, and
# the agent loop reads that as "I am done, here is the answer" (see AgentRepl in
# packages/repl/src/repl.ts). So the form count starts at zero, not one.
root    ::= prose (topform prose)*

# Free text around the forms: anything but a parenthesis, a "<" or a "[". Every
# chat model brackets its control tags with one of those two: <think> </think>
# (deepseek, qwen, seed-oss), <|channel>thought <channel|> (gemma-4), <|start|>
# <|channel|> <|message|> (gpt-oss), [THINK] [TOOL_CALLS] (mistral). All of them
# are paren-free, so all of them used to pass straight through here as prose —
# and when a thinking model opens one, the whole reply is spent inside it: the
# trace is decoded under this grammar so it comes out as strained pseudo-Lisp,
# it eats the token budget, and the turn can end with the content empty and no
# program at all. Banning the two characters every tag begins with makes the
# whole family unsayable at decode time, whatever tokens a model spells them
# with, and fails closed on models we have not met yet. The cost is that prose
# cannot hold a literal "<" or "[": write "less than", or put the comparison in
# a form, (< 1 2). The one gap is functionary's ">>>all" — ">" is too useful in
# prose to spend on a model we do not run.
prose   ::= [^()<\[]*

# Only a (possibly quoted) list is a top-level form — a bare atom out here
# would be indistinguishable from prose.
topform ::= (",@" | "'" | "`" | ",")* list

form    ::= list | quoted | string | atom

# Reader macros: 'x  `x  ,x  ,@x
quoted  ::= (",@" | "'" | "`" | ",") ws form

# Proper or dotted list: (a b c) or (a b . c)
list    ::= "(" ws (form ws)* ("." ws form ws)? ")"

# Symbols, numbers (int/bigint/float), keywords (:kw), nil and t all share one
# lexical class: a run of any char except whitespace, the structural chars
# ( ) " the quote chars ' ` , and ~. A lone "." is excluded — the reader only
# ever treats a bare "." as the dotted-pair marker, never as a symbol — while
# atoms that merely contain "." (e.g. .30, 3.14, fs/read) stay valid.
atom    ::= hchar achar* | "." achar+
hchar   ::= [^ \t\r\n()'\"`,~.]
achar   ::= [^ \t\r\n()'\"`,~]

# Double-quoted string with backslash escapes.
string  ::= "\"" schar* "\""
schar   ::= [^\"\\] | "\\" .

ws      ::= [ \t\r\n]*
